A perfect hash function, found by search
========================================

The program space is three operations with a few constants, nine
alternatives per position; `program n` is every program of length n, by
nondeterminism; `perfect keys size n` keeps the programs that send the keys
into distinct slots of a table.  go.py asks for lengths 1, 2, 3 and stops
at the first solution: a search for a program in a dozen lines of Curry.
The same search in Python, written out with itertools, runs beside it for
the time (reported on standard error so expected.out is repeatable).

The point is not the hash function.  It is that the enumeration, the
failure of the candidates that collide, and the stop at the first solution
are the language's, so the Curry text states the problem and nothing else.

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first, then "./run"
from this directory.  The file expected.out records the standard output.

The 50 keywords use a table of 512 slots.  At 128 slots none of the
original 15 + 225 + 3375 programs of lengths one through three is perfect.
The shift constants are now 2 and 4: nine operations per position, with
9 programs at length one and 81 at length two.  At 512 slots length one
has no solution; length two finds [Mul 31, Shr 2].  The Python enumeration
finds the same program, its fifth candidate at length two.  A first C++
run took 1.54 seconds including compilation and startup; Python's brute
force took 0.000221 seconds; Curry's two searches took 0.71 seconds.
Times vary.

The example test uses C++: the reference backend was still searching
length two when stopped at 60 seconds.
